DataFrame 1:N Series

Blk = ZuUnion<Hdr, ZmRef<Buf>>

Series 1:N Blk {
  string name		// name

  ID seriesID		// seriesID is 0-based within store

    // series register buffer unloadFn with store, obtaining seriesID which
    // indexes array of unloadFns; these are used to revert a Blk from a
    // ZmRef<Buf> that is being unloaded back to just the header
}

Buf : Hdr

Hdr { // in-memory
  uint64 offset;	// offset of this block (in values)
  int64 last		// last value in block
  uint32 blkID;		// block ID (from 0)
  uint16 count		// count of values in block
  uint8 ndp		// NDP for this block
}
// 24 bytes

assuming ~50% compression ratio, 4K block would be 512 values
assuming 10K per second, 1 second is 10 blocks , or 240bytes in hdrs,
or 19M for an entire day in memory, 570M for a month, this is about
right

// while appending
Hdr {
  uint28 length		// length in bytes of block (so far)
}

// loaded buffer (current implementation)
Buf {
  void *mgr;		// pointer to store
  unsigned seriesID;	// seriesID that owns this block
  unsigned blkIndex;	// block number in series
  Hdr, data
}

// --------------

DF: id, epoch
Series: id (matches field)
Hdr: <as above>, 
Blk: |dfID, seriesID, blkID| data

///

essentially the series loads all blk headers into an in-memory array
at startup and maintains that as it extends the series; it uses
interpolation search (ZuInterSearch) to seek forward and backwards
in the blk array, using seekFn() to score the distance to move to
find the block containing the desired offset; intra-block searching
is done forwards by the block decoder;

offset and count are used for this purpose and must be in-memory;
ndp and last are used by value find (see findFn); 
